 lchingen  staff    1920 Nov 20 17:31 round7.md\ndrwxr-xr-x@  5 lchingen  staff     160 Nov 20 17:30 run\n-rw-r--r--@  1 lchingen  staff     339 Nov 20 17:29 send.log\nold-2 control 1: rung 3 refused False spent $0.0032\nold-2 changed 1: rung 3 refused False spent $0.0076\nold-2 control 2: rung 3 refused False spent $0.0098\nold-2 changed 2: rung 3 refused False spent $0.0133\nold-2 control 3: rung 3 refused False spent $0.0159\nold-2 changed 3: rung 3 refused False spent $0.0176\ntotal spent $0.0176\nexit 0\n# Claude's marks, written before the blind reader (reading-rule.md, A to E)\n\nv14's 09-20 figures: FY $419B, Q3 $113.5B, Q4 $127B. Room floors (D): Data Center FY at most $394B, Q3 at most $107.5B, Q4 at most\n$121B. A stated range is read at its midpoint (the rule did not say; the lower-bound reading is given beside it and changes no verdict,\nbecause C fails those answers anyway).\n\n| Answer | A: FY / Q3 / Q4 | B adds up | C grounded | D room (midpoint) | E | Reconciles |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| control 1 | $389–394B / $100B / $125–130B | yes | no: Q4 is \"an allowance for the stated Q4 acceleration\", which it says is not quantified | Q4 $127.5B > $121B: no (lower bound $125B: no) | n/a | **no** |\n| control 2 | $389–394B / $99.9B / $125–130B | yes | no: same unquantified Q4 step | Q4 $127.5B: no (lower $125B: no) | n/a | **no** |\n| control 3 | $384–390B / $100B / $120–126B | yes | no: \"management's Q4 comments support\" a figure they never size | Q4 $123B: no (lower $120B: yes) | n/a | **no** |\n| changed 1 | $387–390B / $105B / $118B | yes (164 + 105 + 118 = 387) | yes: v14's Q3 $113.5B and Q4 $127B times the 92.5% mix in the request | FY room $30.5B, Q3 $8.5B, Q4 $9B: yes | used | **yes** |\n| changed 2 | $384–389B / $100B / $120–125B | yes | no: the same unquantified Q4 step as the controls | Q4 $122.5B: no (lower $120B: yes) | noticed (\"consistent with the dependent FY2027 total-revenue conclusion\") | **no** |\n| changed 3 | $382B / $99.9B / $117.5B | yes (164.25 + 99.9 + 117.5 = 381.65) | yes: v14's Q4 $127B times the 92.5% mix | FY room $37B, Q3 $13.6B, Q4 $9.5B: yes | used | **yes** |\n\n**Tally:** changed 2 of 3 reconcile, control 0 of 3, no refusal in either arm (all first attempts accepted). By the rule as written:\n**PASS**.\n\n**What else the answers show (not part of the rule):**\n- **The stored contradiction was partly one unlucky sample.** All six answers are rung 3 at about $382–394B. None repeats the stored\n  09-20 answer (rung 2, about $410B, Q4 $146B). The contradiction that remains in the controls is milder: Q4 Data Center of\n  $120–130B against a company-wide Q4 of $127B, leaving the rest of NVIDIA $0–7B where it earned $6.35B and $7.2B in Q1 and Q2.\n- **What the link changed:** two of three changed answers sized Q4 from v14's figures and the reported mix instead of an unsized\n  \"acceleration\". Changed 1 also rejected the self-made $414B reference and derived about $380B from the $411B consensus; the other\n  two kept \"$414B\" as the reference, as all three controls did.\n- **Coherence is not correctness:** the changed answers inherit v14's own assumption (Q4 at Q3's 12% growth).\nS and U reconcile.\n\nAll figures are in billions of dollars. A and D list FY2027, Q3, then Q4. Parentheses in A give the lower estimate. Parentheses in D give the room at that lower estimate.\n\n| Answer | A · Data Center estimate | B · Adds up within $2B | C · Support for the calculation | D · Room for the rest of NVIDIA | E · Use of total-revenue forecast | Reconciles |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P | 387 (384), 100, 123 (120) | Yes | No. Faster hyperscaler growth and rising Rubin supply do not quantify Q4 at $120–126B. | 32 (35), 13.5, 4 (7). Q4 fails at midpoint. | No use shown | No |\n| Q | 386.5 (384), 100, 122.5 (120) | Yes | No. The same growth and supply evidence does not quantify Q4 at $120–125B. | 32.5 (35), 13.5, 4.5 (7). Q4 fails at midpoint. | Noticed only | No |\n| R | 391.5 (389), 100, 127.5 (125) | Yes | No. The carried-forward growth rate is unspecified, and the extra Q4 acceleration is unquantified. | 27.5 (30), 13.5, −0.5 (2). Q4 fails even at the lower estimate. | No use shown | No |\n| S | 388.5 (387), 105, 118 | Yes | Yes. Applies the reported 92.5% share to the quoted Q3 and Q4 totals. | 30.5 (32), 8.5, 9. Pass. | Used in Q3 and Q4 | Yes |\n| T | 391.5 (389), 99.9, 127.5 (125) | Yes | No. Demand and the Rubin ramp do not quantify Q4 at $125–130B. | 27.5 (30), 13.6, −0.5 (2). Q4 fails even at the lower estimate. | No use shown | No |\n| U | 382, 99.9, 117.5 | Yes | Yes. Uses the $108B Q3 guide and the quoted $127B Q4 estimate with the reported 92.5% share. | 37, 13.6, 9.5. Pass. | Used in Q4 | Yes |\n\nThe $414B market reference appears in the previous conclusion without a source establishing it as a Data Center forecast.  \nS and U carry the latest 92.5% share into Q4, where the actual share could differ.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r '[.arm,.sample,.attempts[-1].answer.rung,.attempts[-1].answer.reference,.attempts[-1].answer.implied_outcome,.attempts[-1].answer.reasons, (.attempts[-1].answer.cites_views|join(\\\",\\\"))] | @tsv' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/answers.jsonl; cat docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/reading-rule.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq -r '[.arm,.sample,.attempts[-1].answer.rung,.attempts[-1].answer.reference,.attempts[-1].answer.implied_outcome,.attempts[-1].answer.reasons, (.attempts[-1].answer.cites_views|join(\\\",\\\"))] | @tsv' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/answers.jsonl; cat docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/reading-rule.md\"","aggregated_output":"control\t1\t3\tThe market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2.\tAbout $389–394 billion: $164.25 billion reported in Q1–Q2, approximately $100 billion implied for Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and roughly $125–130 billion for Q4 by carrying forward guided growth while allowing for the stated Q4 acceleration as Rubin supply builds.\tThe market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The implied outcome is about $389–394 billion: reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totals $164.25 billion; applying the latest total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix implies about $100 billion in Q3; and carrying the guided sequential growth forward, with an allowance for the stated Q4 acceleration as Rubin supply builds, implies roughly $125–130 billion in Q4. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and the Rubin ramp lift the estimate versus the prior conclusion, but the Q4 acceleration is not quantified and the evidence also indicates supply constraints; the reported first-half annualized run rate alone is not a full-year forecast.\t\nchanged\t1\t3\tAbout $411 billion of FY2027 total NVIDIA revenue, which is rung 2 for the total-revenue thesis; at the latest Data Center mix of roughly 92.5%, that implies about $380 billion of Data Center revenue, rung 3.\tAbout $387–390 billion: H1 Data Center revenue was about $164 billion; applying the latest roughly 92.5% Data Center mix to v14's estimated Q3 total revenue of about $113.5 billion and Q4 total revenue of about $127 billion gives roughly $105 billion in Q3 and $118 billion in Q4. The result is below $400 billion and above $350 billion.\tThe market reference is about $411 billion of FY2027 total NVIDIA revenue; applying the latest Data Center mix of roughly 92.5% puts the Data Center reference near $380 billion, at rung 3. The implied Data Center outcome is modestly higher, but remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary: H1 was about $164 billion, with Q3 and Q4 estimates of roughly $105 billion and $118 billion, respectively. Strong demand and the Rubin ramp support those estimates, while supply constraints and the lack of a quantified FY2027 contribution from multiyear deployment plans limit the case for crossing $400 billion.\tv14\ncontrol\t2\t3\tThe market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, placing it at rung 2 (at least $400 billion and below $450 billion).\tAbout $389–394 billion: $164.25 billion reported in Q1–Q2, approximately $99.9 billion for Q3 using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, plus an estimated $125–130 billion in Q4 as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds.\tThe market reference remains approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence now implies about $389–394 billion: Q1 and Q2 contributed $164.25 billion, Q3 is about $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is roughly $125–130 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That estimate is higher than the previous $385–390 billion view, but remains below the $400 billion rung boundary. Strong demand, Rubin orders, and customer utilization support the upper end; the 2027–2028 nature of some commitments, supply constraints, and no China compute revenue in the forward outlook limit the uplift.\t\nchanged\t2\t3\tApproximately $414 billion for FY2027, the supplied market reference; this sits at rung 2 (at least $400 billion and below $450 billion).\tAbout $384–389 billion: the first two quarters total about $164 billion, Q3 is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds.\tThe market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The operating evidence still implies a lower result: the first half contributed about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is about $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent segment mix, and Q4 is roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That yields about $384–389 billion, below the $400 billion boundary. Strong demand and the Rubin ramp support growth, but the evidence does not quantify enough Q4 revenue to bridge the gap to rung 2; the segment estimate is also consistent with the dependent FY2027 total-revenue conclusion.\tv14\ncontrol\t3\t3\tApproximately $414 billion for FY2027, the market reference supplied in the previous conclusion; this is rung 2 (at least $400 billion and below $450 billion).\tAbout $384–390 billion: approximately $164 billion in the first half, about $100 billion in Q3 from the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent 92.5% Data Center mix, plus roughly $120–126 billion in Q4 as supply builds and hyperscale growth reaccelerates.\tThe market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The evidence implies about $384–390 billion: Q1 and Q2 total about $164 billion; applying the latest Data Center mix to NVIDIA’s $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide implies about $100 billion for Q3; and management’s Q4 comments support roughly $120–126 billion as Rubin supply builds and hyperscale growth reaccelerates. This is below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary and within rung 3. Demand and customer commitments support growth, but the no-China-revenue outlook, supply constraints, and earlier competition weigh against lifting the estimate above that boundary.\t\nchanged\t3\t3\tApproximately $414 billion for FY2027, the supplied market reference; this sits at rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion).\tApproximately $382 billion: $164.25 billion reported in the first half, about $99.9 billion implied for Q3, and about $117.5 billion for Q4 by applying the recent 92.5% Data Center mix to v14’s roughly $127 billion Q4 total-revenue estimate.\tThe market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The reported first-half Data Center revenue is about $164.25 billion; Q3 is implied at about $99.9 billion using NVIDIA’s $108 billion total-revenue guide and the recent 92.5% Data Center mix. For Q4, applying that mix to the roughly $127 billion total-revenue estimate in v14 gives about $117.5 billion, for implied FY2027 Data Center revenue of approximately $382 billion. That is below the $400 billion boundary for rung 2 and within rung 3. Rubin shipments, orders, and expected Q4 hyperscaler reacceleration support upside, but do not provide a quantified FY2027 increment sufficient to bridge the gap; the reported supply constraint is a counterweight. The implied Data Center total also remains below v14’s whole-company revenue estimate.\tv14\n# Council Q, rounds 4–5: the probe after your round-3 and round-4 changes (read-only; no edits, no model calls)\n\nYour round 3 (`codex-r3.md`, DISAGREE) asked for four changes. All are taken:\n1. **old-2 only decides.** `probe.py` sends only old-2 (`SEND_DRAWS`): control (the stored 09-20 request of v9, byte-equal) and\n   changed (plus v14's conclusion through one `first_is_bullish` dependency, production's 600-character block), three fresh samples\n   each: 6 sends. old-1 and old-3 are not sent; `render-check.json` records them as examples of what the cut loses.\n2. **Harness.** A sample's archive path that exists refuses the send; both attempts of a refused answer are kept; before each call the\n   worst case is reserved against a cap of $0.15 (revised in round 5, below).\n3. **The reading rule below, the same for both arms.**\n4. **Use of the linked forecast tracked apart.**\n\n## The reading rule (fixed before any answer)\nFor each answer, two readers (Claude first, then you blind to the arm under shuffled labels, given the answer, v14's 09-20 conclusion\nand the request's evidence) record:\n- **A.** Its FY2027 Data Center estimate and its Q3 and Q4 FY2027 Data Center estimates, as stated or as its own arithmetic gives them.\n- **B.** Whether its arithmetic adds up (its quarters sum to its year within $2B).\n- **C.** Whether each assumption is disclosed and grounded in its own request (a figure, guidance or growth rate that request\n  contains: the evidence listed, and v14's shown conclusion only in the changed arm); a step with no source in its request is\n  unsupported.\n- **D.** Room for the rest of NVIDIA: v14's figures minus the Data Center figures leave at least $25B for FY2027 and at least $6B\n  in each of Q3 and Q4. These floors are this test's assumptions, taken from the reported non-Data-Center revenue in the request\n  (Q1 $6.35B, Q2 $7.2B, first half $13.55B). Both arms are measured against v14's 09-20 figures ($419B FY, $113.5B Q3, $127B Q4),\n  whether or not the answer saw them.\n- **E.** (changed arm only, apart) whether v14's forecast bounds or changes its calculation (a v14 figure used in its arithmetic or\n  as a stated ceiling); a citation or a mention alone is recorded as \"noticed\", not \"used\".\n\n**Reconciles** = A gives all three figures, B, C and D all hold. A missing Q3 or Q4 estimate = \"not tested\", never a pass.\n\n- **Pass:** changed reconciles in at least 2 of 3; control reconciles in at most 1 of 3; no added refusal (first attempts compared).\n- **Inconclusive:** control reconciles in 2 or 3 of 3 (the stored contradiction is a coin flip here), or changed has 2 or more\n  \"not tested\".\n- **Fail:** otherwise. A fail says this input did not reliably help; it does not locate the cause.\n- Where the two readers differ on a point, the source decides, both marks recorded.\n\n**What it decides:** pass → reopen the dependency design review (D542's five items, the `reads`/`supports` separation, D538's seven\nfiling changes) with the council, then Alex; fail or inconclusive → report to Alex, no further send without a new design.\n\n## Round 5: your round-4 changes, all taken\n- Q3 recorded under A and held under D ($113.5B less $6B); the $25B and $6B floors stated as this test's assumptions.\n- C credits only support its own request contained; E separates \"used\" (bounds or changes the arithmetic) from \"noticed\".\n- The cap reserves one token per character plus the full output allowance, times the transport's three attempts (about $0.047 a\n  call); 6 sends and their possible retries stay under $0.15.\n\nIs this now sound to send? End with CONSENSUS or DISAGREE. Under 150 words.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
