{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"**PASS for the registered test. Keep the PARTLY verdict and reopen the dependency design review.** Two of three linked answers reconciled, versus zero of three controls, with no refusals. Both passing answers used v14’s $127B company-wide Q4 forecast and the reported 92.5% Data Center share to get roughly $118B. The link gave them a basis for sizing Q4. The quarter sums already added up in all six answers. [Raw answers](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/run/answers.jsonl)\n\n**The $146B Q4 estimate didn’t recur.** All six answers landed in the $350–400B band, so the probe showed no change in the annual answer band. Six reruns leave the frequency of the extreme error unknown. The milder problem persisted: at their midpoints, the controls left between −$0.5B and $4B for the rest of NVIDIA in Q4. The passing answers inherit v14’s assumed Q4 growth and carry forward the prior quarter’s segment share. That’s enough to explain the improved fit, while forecast accuracy remains open. [Reader marks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-probe/claude-marks.md)\n\n**Keep the daily wrapper deferred.** The library is still useful for finding sources and checking milestones. Reopen the dependency review as registered, including the risk of removing evidence from the company-wide card. Track the $414B reference separately: five of six answers carried it forward, including one that reconciled. That makes it a live defect, and it can be traced in stored answers without delaying this review. [D538](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6479)\n\n**CONSENSUS**"}}
